WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.121 --> 00:00:13.286
Old Behrman was a painter who lived on the ground floor beneath them.

2
00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:19.129
He was over sixty years old, had the body of an elf, and a long beard curling down

3
00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:19.889
from his chin.

4
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.491
Behrman was a failure as an artist.

5
00:00:23.131 --> 00:00:28.542
For forty years he had tried to paint, but he had never gotten one job to illustrate even

6
00:00:28.602 --> 00:00:30.163
a simple magazine story.

7
00:00:30.843 --> 00:00:35.165
He had always been about to paint a masterpiece, but had never started it.

8
00:00:35.646 --> 00:00:39.628
He earned a little as a model for young artists in the colony who could not pay for

9
00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:40.688
a professional model.

10
00:00:41.289 --> 00:00:46.572
He drank too much gin and always talked of the great masterpiece he was going to paint someday.

11
00:00:47.192 --> 00:00:53.716
To many people, he was a fierce little old man who scoffed at softness or sentiment in anyone.

12
00:00:54.284 --> 00:00:59.547
But he protected the two young women artists in the studio above him like a guard dog.

13
00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:01.648
Sue found Mr.

14
00:01:01.688 --> 00:01:05.730
Behrman smelling strongly of gin in his dimly lit den below.

15
00:01:06.611 --> 00:01:11.393
In one corner of the room was a blank canvas on an easel; it had been waiting there

16
00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:15.295
for 25 years for the old man to start the creation of his masterpiece.

17
00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:22.619
Sue told him of Johnsy's irrational fantasy about the ivy on the wall, and how she feared Johnsy

18
00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:27.150
would lose her hold on life when the last leaf lost its hold on the vine.

19
00:01:28.111 --> 00:01:33.035
His eyes red, old Behrman shouted his contempt for such idiotic imaginings.

20
00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:34.937
"Well!" he cried.

21
00:01:34.957 --> 00:01:40.601
"I can't believe there are such foolish people in the world that would die because a leaf drops

22
00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:41.802
off a stupid vine.

23
00:01:42.643 --> 00:01:44.144
I've never heard of such a thing.

24
00:01:44.685 --> 00:01:45.165
And no!

25
00:01:45.525 --> 00:01:47.467
I will not be a model for your hermit!

26
00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:51.050
Why are you allowing these silly ideas to come into her head?

27
00:01:51.671 --> 00:01:53.212
Poor little Miss Johnsy."

28
00:01:54.352 --> 00:01:59.775
"She is very ill and weak," said Sue, "and the fever has left her mind dark and full

29
00:01:59.795 --> 00:02:00.855
of strange ideas.

30
00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:02.636
Very well, Mr.

31
00:02:02.656 --> 00:02:06.398
Behrman, if you do not care to pose for me, you don't have to.

32
00:02:07.118 --> 00:02:09.439
But I think you are a horrid old man."

33
00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:11.860
"You are just like a woman!"

34
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.121
yelled Behrman.

35
00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:14.921
"Who said I wouldn't pose?

36
00:02:15.362 --> 00:02:15.822
Go on.

37
00:02:15.862 --> 00:02:16.642
I'll come with you.

38
00:02:17.322 --> 00:02:20.544
I've been trying to tell you for half an hour that I was ready to pose.

39
00:02:21.344 --> 00:02:26.306
For heaven's sake, Miss Johnsy shouldn't be lying sick in a drafty old place like this.

40
00:02:27.207 --> 00:02:32.769
Someday, I will paint my masterpiece, and we shall all leave here for someplace warm and sunny."

41
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.510
Johnsy was sleeping when they went upstairs.

42
00:02:36.251 --> 00:02:40.733
Sue pulled the shade down to the windowsill and motioned Behrman into the other room.

43
00:02:41.613 --> 00:02:46.595
They peered out the window fearfully at the ivy vine, and looked at each other for a moment

44
00:02:46.655 --> 00:02:47.456
without speaking.

45
00:02:48.508 --> 00:02:52.632
A persistent, cold rain was falling, mingled with snow.

46
00:02:53.572 --> 00:03:00.518
Behrman, in his old blue shirt, took his seat as the hermit miner on a large upside-down waste

47
00:03:00.558 --> 00:03:02.960
basket, which stood in for a rock.

48
00:03:03.861 --> 00:03:08.725
He stayed and posed for Sue late into the stormy evening, and then left.

49
00:03:09.505 --> 00:03:15.991
When Sue awoke the next morning, she found Johnsy with wide-open eyes staring at the drawn shade.

50
00:03:16.011 --> 00:03:20.237
"Pull it up, I want to see," she ordered, in a whisper.

51
00:03:20.877 --> 00:03:22.738
Wearily, Sue obeyed.

52
00:03:22.758 --> 00:03:24.159
"Johnsy, look!"

53
00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:25.339
cried Sue.

54
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:32.822
After the beating rain and fierce gusts of wind that had raged through the entire night, standing out

55
00:03:32.882 --> 00:03:35.844
against the brick wall was one ivy leaf.

56
00:03:36.904 --> 00:03:38.545
It was the last one on the vine.

57
00:03:39.345 --> 00:03:45.508
Still dark green near its stem, with its serrated edges tinted with the yellow of death and decay,

58
00:03:46.236 --> 00:03:49.658
it hung bravely from the branch some twenty feet above the ground.

59
00:03:49.678 --> 00:03:52.520
"It's the last one," said Johnsy.

60
00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.301
"I thought it would surely fall during the night.

61
00:03:55.861 --> 00:03:56.702
I heard the wind.

62
00:03:57.462 --> 00:04:00.984
It will fall today, and I shall die at the same time."

63
00:04:01.004 --> 00:04:09.049
"Oh, Johnsy," said Sue, leaning her worn face down toward the head on the pillow, "think of me,

64
00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:10.890
if you won't think of yourself.

65
00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:13.191
What would I do without you?"

66
00:04:13.651 --> 00:04:15.092
But Johnsy did not answer.

67
00:04:15.692 --> 00:04:20.075
The loneliest thing in the world is a soul when it is getting ready to go on its

68
00:04:20.155 --> 00:04:21.876
mysterious, far journey.

69
00:04:22.597 --> 00:04:28.561
The idea seemed to possess Johnsy more strongly as one by one the ties that bound her to

70
00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.742
friendship and to earth untangled.

71
00:04:31.743 --> 00:04:37.727
The day wore away, and even through the twilight, the two women could see the lone ivy leaf

72
00:04:38.107 --> 00:04:40.148
clinging to its stem against the wall.

73
00:04:41.129 --> 00:04:47.577
But then, with the coming of night, the north wind blew fierce again, while the rain still beat

74
00:04:47.617 --> 00:04:50.959
against the windows and pattered down from the low eaves.

75
00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.643
When it was light enough, Johnsy demanded that the shade be raised.

76
00:04:56.223 --> 00:04:57.884
The ivy leaf was still there.

77
00:04:58.525 --> 00:05:01.206
Johnsy lay for a long time looking at it.

78
00:05:01.987 --> 00:05:06.630
And then she called to Sue, who was stirring her chicken broth over the gas stove.

79
00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.132
"I've been a bad girl, Sue," she said.

80
00:05:10.152 --> 00:05:15.276
"Something has made that last leaf stay there to show me how wicked I was.

81
00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:17.813
It is a sin to want to die.

82
00:05:18.413 --> 00:05:23.036
I'd like a little broth now and some milk with a bit of wine in it, if you

83
00:05:23.056 --> 00:05:23.556
don't mind.

84
00:05:24.396 --> 00:05:26.397
And I'll sit up in bed and watch you cook."

85
00:05:27.458 --> 00:05:33.041
An hour later she said, "Sue, some day I hope to paint the Bay of Naples."

86
00:05:34.002 --> 00:05:39.024
Just then, the doctor arrived, and Sue made an excuse to go into the hallway as he left.

87
00:05:39.044 --> 00:05:45.868
"Johnsy's got a good chance now," said the doctor, taking Sue's thin, shaking hand in his.

88
00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.221
"With good nursing, she'll be fine.

89
00:05:48.821 --> 00:05:51.202
And now I must see another case downstairs.

90
00:05:51.922 --> 00:05:54.943
Behrman, his name is—some kind of artist, I believe.

91
00:05:55.604 --> 00:05:56.864
He has pneumonia too.

92
00:05:57.404 --> 00:06:00.906
He is an old, weak man, and there is no hope for him.

93
00:06:01.686 --> 00:06:05.588
But he's going to the hospital this morning, so he'll be more comfortable."

94
00:06:06.248 --> 00:06:12.971
That evening, before the sun set, Sue came to the bed where Johnsy lay, contentedly knitting a very

95
00:06:13.071 --> 00:06:16.072
blue woolen scarf, and put one arm around her.

96
00:06:17.268 --> 00:06:19.829
"I have something to tell you, Johnsy," she said.

97
00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:23.410
"Mr. Behrman died of pneumonia today in the hospital.

98
00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:28.271
The janitor found him this morning in his room downstairs helpless with pain.

99
00:06:29.011 --> 00:06:32.372
His shoes and clothing were wet through and icy cold.

100
00:06:32.972 --> 00:06:36.093
They couldn't imagine where he had been on such a dreadful night.

101
00:06:36.753 --> 00:06:44.015
And then they found a lantern, still lit; a ladder; some scattered brushes; and a palette with green

102
00:06:44.116 --> 00:06:45.656
and yellow colors mixed on it.

103
00:06:46.464 --> 00:06:49.366
Look out the window at the last ivy leaf on the wall.

104
00:06:49.906 --> 00:06:53.408
Didn't you wonder why it never fluttered or moved when the wind blew?

105
00:06:54.389 --> 00:06:57.131
Ah, darling, it's Behrman's masterpiece.

106
00:06:57.751 --> 00:07:00.753
He painted it there the night that the last leaf fell."
